{"kind":"report","text":"Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED. Checker review of J4 later-phases Source V12 at operator 1bf5b420 and evidence body 4997254d, for review request 3ee5e457. I don't adopt V12.\n\nThe phase order, the once claims, signing and custody all hold, and I found no way to run a phase twice or sign an original twice. What fails is the public record after a halt, plus one field that can reach public output unchecked. This Source runs once, so fix these before names and assembly. The operator's digest changes, so the next Source needs a new qualification, a rehearsal and the affected controls.\n\n## Changes I request\n\n1. Name every live credential in public, on every path. Ruling 8f2f7140 item 5 says a leftover goes in public with its state, and that the planner handles live tokens afterwards. `result.leftovers` exists only in `rehold` and `cleanup`, and a halt saves no state. So on a `work` halt:\n   - The minted workspace tokens have no ID, number or end in public. `result.credentials` holds only the remote.\n   - The room read token, one hour on admin's account, is named on no path, and that includes a clean `cleanup`. This answers your point a: it needs a row.\n   - The independent token appears only in the sibling `independent-read-provenance.json`, and not at all if `parseIndependent` refuses. `result.independentReadCleanup` is set only after `end-a` succeeds.\n   - A token whose revoke is refused or unknown in `end-a` has no ID, because `result.revocation` comes later.\n   - A commit pushed before a stop, and any ref written by an accepted cross-fork, room or old-token push, has no name. Only the POST appears in `gitResponses`.\n   Keep `result.leftovers` current as each step completes, so that the existing `finally` writes it on a halt. Add a rehearsal scenario that reads these rows after a halt at `push-a`. A killed process still writes no `result.json`, so say that in the limits.\n\n2. Check `reviewBasis` before it goes public. `parseIndependent` requires the key and never reads its value, and `operator.mjs:313` copies the whole attestation to public output. Whatever the planner's file holds there goes out verbatim. Give it a closed form, a digest or fixed word, with a qualifier case.\n\n3. Read the admin's actions before the first original. `operator.mjs:203` checks `rules.activate` and `issue.open`. `review-source` and `merge` are signed by admin and need `change.review` and `change.merge`. The default table in `membership.ts:106-107` gives both, but no public file shows this room's roster. A narrower roster would refuse at `review`, after the holds and pushes are spent. Add both to the `rehold` read, before `rehold-a`.\n\n4. Correct three statements that the code doesn't support:\n   - PLAN says that `setup-result.mjs` closes every object. It doesn't: `actors`, `rulesObservation`, `nativeObservation`, `rulesExchange`, `setupCleanup`, `taskEvidence`, `finalGate.sourceEquivalence`, `deployment.evidence`, `network.bounds` and `network.counts` have no member check. The digest pin c99c942d is what protects this file, so say that.\n   - `collector.py:190` says that recovery checks the receipt, stdout and result closure. For a later phase, `parentOf` reads only the state file. If the collector dies after the operator saves state, no `HALTED.json` exists and the next phase runs. Make this a named release read: before each release, read the previous phase's receipt and confirm that it is complete with exit 0, no timeout, and the classification `\u003cphase\u003e-complete`. That also covers `rehold-complete-leftovers-open`, which exits 0 and which `work` accepts.\n   - Open decision 1 doesn't say when. The admission binds the private binding by digest (`recovery.mjs:161,185`), so an edit after admission halts with `later-admission-changed`. `independentForkReadFile` must be settled before admission.\n\n5. Make the independent token's margin match its use. `work` requires more than 300 s left, but `positiveRead` needs more than 15 s left at its last read, which can come at second 300. Require budget plus 15. A short token halts here and can't pass falsely, so this only moves the refusal to before the first original.\n\n## Your points a to i\n\n- a. Item 1 above.\n- b. I accept 300 s. Your arithmetic gives 208 s plus two pushes, and the bound ends in a halt with nothing false written.\n- c. I accept the collector changes, which match the phase and label substitutions in the patch. The credential scan `noCredentialShape` covers only the setup result, and nothing scans later `result.json` or stdout during a live run. It also misses `Session \u003ctoken\u003e` readers and tokens without the `token_` prefix. In the rehearsal, a harness scan for `standin_` tokens over the capture and stdout came back clean, and the reader traced every public write and found no plaintext path other than item 2. So I accept this, but don't describe it as a live scan.\n- d. I accept the four unpaired scenarios. `fork-refused` and `fork-unknown` reuse V11's controlled words, and the other two end on words that controlled scenarios also reach.\n- e. For the planner, below.\n- f. I accept it. It follows ruling 01b584e8 item 6.\n- g. Item 4, and the planner's to confirm.\n- h. This is what `rehold` exists to observe. The local paired control is good evidence of the judge and nothing more.\n- i. No action.\n\n## Decisions for the planner\n\n1. A cross-fork or room push that answers 404 halts the namespace. `git.mjs:35` accepts only 401 or 403, even when the read-backs show no ref was written. Nobody has seen the real provider's answer to a token for another repository, and I can't tell it from this Source. Ruling 92167206 names 401 or 403 for the old write token only. I recommend keeping that exact rule for the old token, and letting the two probes accept 404 too when the read-back shows no ref, recorded as observed. Otherwise an unknown status word can spend the room at `push-a`. If you want this, it goes in the next Source.\n\n2. A refused revoke at `cleanup`. The real provider's answer to revoking an expired token is unknown, and `artifacts-host.ts` treats `false` as refused. `cleanup` then halts after `publish` has made its claim. You can avoid the question: b's token lasts 900 s from `mint-b`, and by arithmetic `manifest`, `review`, `publish` and `cleanup` need about 28 s plus 20 s of SDK load. Holding all the releases ready before `rehold` keeps the token live at `cleanup`.\n\n3. The independent token's read scope rests on your channel. `scope` in that file is self-declared and no write probe runs, so the operator can't tell a write token from a read token. Only `remote` and `repository` meet a value from outside the two planner files.\n\n4. `process_owner.py` sets no `env=`, and the rehearsal itself uses `NODE_OPTIONS` to redirect origins with exact argv. Add a release read: Root's shell has no `NODE_OPTIONS`, `NODE_DEBUG` or preload.\n\n## What I read and ran\n\nI read PLAN's V12 section, `later.mjs`, `later-native.mjs`, `operator.mjs` lines 262-476 and 480-end, and the provider's revoke path at 112eff12. An independent reader, which I gave the code without my conclusions, read `recovery.mjs`, `collector.py`, `setup-result.mjs`, `safety.mjs`, `git.mjs`, `assemble.py`, `finish-later.py`, `packet_plan.py` and both fresh-names files, and tested route rows against 112eff12. I then checked at source the findings I rely on here, but not each of its other readings.\n\nThe reader found these route rows sound: L02, L12, L13, L14, L15, L21 and L28. It reached no native rule bodies for L07 to L10, L22, L24, L25, L27 or L29, and `qualify-source.mjs` and `rehearse-later.mjs` got only a partial read. It found nothing in `assemble.py`, `finish-later.py` or `packet_plan.py`.\n\nThe evidence verifies. All 46 index rows match, and the source-index, PINS, receipt and controls digests match the request. The read-only copy's `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 9810d1fe, and no file there is writable. The receipt shows all 19 scenarios at their expected ends, and all 27 control rows report `controlShowsCheck`. I read those rows and ran no control, no rehearsal and no qualification.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: the phase chain and one-command `work` follow the rulings. Sound in design. It waits on items 1 and 3.\n- Security: no private value reaches public output by any path that the reader or I traced, except `reviewBasis` in item 2. Not adopted until items 1 and 2 are fixed.\n- Simplification: no objection. Items 1 to 3 add fields and checks to existing paths, and they add no phase.\n\nThis review releases nothing. It is not J4 or S1 completion.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0448dfa6b8fe54df8058bc0f4ed9e9435de9b29e stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e0dd696d953e8a4209c9e0a7461afc19c30b9a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:043d587ef18b62fdeee6b57f8968de1567206697, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:070231aadcb17348891458bc2eedb5bbf529860c, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:171f5f57d26458ff1e85c1f9e0a69fefb342bc9b and 19 more"}}